if you are drinking coffee this morning  we're mainlining it like we are you may  not be thinking about what it took to  get those beans from crop to cup but the  man you're about to hear from sure did  moukhtar Alton chao-li lived through a  harrowing journey and a dramatic escape  to bring one of the oldest coffees from  Yemen to the rest of the world his story  is now being told in a new book by  celebrated author author Dave Eggers CBS  Evening News anchor Jeff Glor sat down  with them at the Hudson Hotel librarian  lounge right here in New York  Mokhtar Alcon shali was 24 and working  as a doorman at a San Francisco  high-rise when he got a delirious idea  leave the Bay Area for Yemen and start a  coffee business what he didn't plan on  was getting trapped in the middle of a  civil war it was a story Dave Eggers  felt compelled to tell I'm always  attracted to stories of self invention  and entrepreneurship and you know self  creation and that's what that's what  he's done  nobody dreams the American Dream better  than immigrants and the sons and  daughters of immigrants Alcon Charlie's  journey is documented by Eggers in the  monk of mocha the port city mocha on the  Red Sea is where the first ever coffee  beans were exported in the 15th century  the amount of pluck and just moukhtar  calls it naive arrogance that goes into  sort of starting a business from scratch  going to Yemen not knowing anything  about coffee cultivation and creating a  business and rallying and organizing all  these farmers around a new idea of a new  way of life it was just incredibly  inspiring naive arrogance yeah I think  the lack of knowledge really helped me  because the in the early days that had  these SWAT diagrams that I would write  up strengths weaknesses opportunities  and threats  so under threats I had things like Al  Qaeda pirates political stability you  know things that any lot of minor yeah  but they standard for any startup you  know Algerian pirates and and you know  looking back at these reports I wrote  myself I'm like boy was I thinking on  the pro side it was this might work the  pro side he was building a bridge  between two worlds Eggers is known for  telling personal stories including his  own his first book a heartbreaking work  of staggering genius was a memoir about  raising his younger brother after losing  both parents to cancer it went to number  one on the New York Times bestseller  list for this book Eggers spent years  listening to Alcon Shelley's story  travelling with him from California to  Africa your story is the most personal  asset that you have and I couldn't have  done it with anyone else to be able to  be that vulnerable Davison was very  caring and very loving and and that made  it much easier to do because some of the  parts of the book are really hilarious  but others we had a lot of tissue paper  during one trip to Yemen Alcon Shelley  found himself trapped as bombs from the  Houthi rebels rained down on the capital  Yemen's Civil War had begun in these  blasts these air strikes there's no  pattern they're just very super attic  and you heard the ground shake and  children crying and it was very hard to  live that moment the people were really  weeping and at one point people stopped  weeping and there was a sort of kind of  tranquility that came about you know we  just we decided just to submit and let  go and know that we're not in control  anymore and that feeling really helped  me a lot and so what was that process  like talking to him and taking him  through your story well in particular  because they've had an idea for side  have started backwards a lot of those  traumatic experiences that just happened  to me just a few weeks before so going  really deep into the pain you know and  to those moments with Dave in a way was  somewhat therapeutic for me it's a good  idea  tough idea but a good idea well we  I always go into it knowing at any point  we can stop and nothing gets printed  without moktari's approval and we meet  when he wants to meet and we talk about  whatever he's comfortable talking about  that's why it takes a long time almost  three years in this case what surprised  you most about the process with him I  guess I came in to coffee as a skeptic  I'd had my first cup of coffee and I was  35 when my daughter was born and I  needed some mental acuity during the day  I needed a different kind of caffeine  and I used it just as fuel I've put a  lot of sugar and just drank it to stay  awake I was still kind of cynical about  the $4 and 75 cent cup of coffee or more  expensive I thought that's ridiculous  that's so pretentious and but then to  sort of backtrack and think like well  what should something cost and and and  the coffee gets picked individually  every cherry has to be chosen by a  picker put in the put in their bag I'm  always thinking of the person who first  picks that back ripe ruby red cherry you  can't get these people out of your mind  the monk of MOCA gradually weaves  together the history of coffee Alcon  shali and his ancestral home three years  later the war there continues it's  perfect situation the single worst  humanitarian crisis in the world 18  million people are food insecure that's  70 percent of the population there's  over 3 million refugees now because of  this war  and for me on one hand I do get and I  feel that I'm helping my farmers make  him make it better living and helping  people learn more about Yemen but I  sometimes do feel that it's a small debt  compared to suffering that's going on  and I know this war will end and I  believe that my project would outlast  these bombs he thinks coffee can heal  division here at home as well there's a  cup of coffee it's crossed cultures and  borders and political hardships find its  way to you and I think it's an  incredible opportunity for us to really  be engage each other for CBS this  morning Saturday Jeff Glor New York I  love what Dave Eggers said at the  beginning of Jeff's story nobody dreams  the American Dream better than  immigrants and kudos to him for drawing  global attention and domestic attention  to the absolute crisis unfolding in  Yemen that is a beautiful story it is
 

